## A. Sentinel-2 Optical Preprocessing Comparison

### Scripts Audited
| Script | Author | Lines | Reader | Resize Method |
|:-------|:-------|------:|:-------|:-------------|
| `preprocess_s2_adarsh.py` | Adarsh | 254 | `rasterio` only | `F.interpolate` (PyTorch bilinear) |
| `preprocess_s2_meet.py` | Meet | 182 | `rasterio` only | `F.interpolate` (PyTorch bilinear) |
| `preprocess_s2_jainam.py` | Jainam | 261 | `PIL.Image.open` (+ `.npy` fallback) | `PIL.Image.resize` bilinear |
| `preprocess_s2_fateh.py` | Fateh | 50 | `PIL.Image.open` (+ `.npy` fallback) | `PIL.Image.resize` bilinear |

### A1. Band Selection & Channel Order

| Script | Bands Read (order) | Final Channel 0 | Channel 1 | Channel 2 |
|:-------|:------------------|:---------------|:----------|:----------|
| `preprocess_s2_adarsh.py` | B02→B03→B04, then `rgb[[2,1,0]]` swap | B04 (Red) ✅ | B03 (Green) ✅ | B02 (Blue) ✅ |
| `preprocess_s2_meet.py` | B04→B03→B02 (direct) | B04 (Red) ✅ | B03 (Green) ✅ | B02 (Blue) ✅ |
| `preprocess_s2_jainam.py` | B04→B03→B02 (default arg) | B04 (Red) ✅ | B03 (Green) ✅ | B02 (Blue) ✅ |
| `preprocess_s2_fateh.py` | B02→B03→B04 (default arg, **no reorder**) | B02 (Blue) ❌ | B03 (Green) ✅ | B04 (Red) ❌ |

> [!CAUTION]
> **`preprocess_s2_fateh.py` has inverted R/B channels** — Channel 0 = Blue instead of Red. When combined with population statistics from `s2_stats.json` (computed as [B04, B03, B02]), this causes **wrong normalization stats applied to wrong channels**. This is a correctness bug for Phase 4.

### A2. Resolution & Resizing

All four scripts: Input 120×120 → Output 224×224. All use bilinear interpolation. ✅ Consistent.

Difference: Adarsh/Meet use `torch.nn.functional.interpolate` (batch dimension required, then squeezed). Jainam/Fateh use `PIL.Image.resize` per-band. Both produce equivalent results but different numerical precision at the sub-pixel level.

### A3. Reflectance Scaling Before Normalization

No script divides raw reflectance values by 10,000 (the standard Sentinel-2 L2A scaling factor to [0,1]). All work in raw Digital Number (DN) space (typical BigEarthNet range: 0–10,000+). **This is consistent across all four implementations** and is the correct approach for BigEarthNet since population statistics (`s2_stats.json`) were computed in raw DN space.

### A4. Normalization Strategy

| Script | Normalization Type | Stats Source | Division-by-zero guard |
|:-------|:------------------|:-------------|:-----------------------|
| `preprocess_s2_adarsh.py` | Population Z-score: `(x - mean) / std` | Loaded from `s2_stats.json` (CWD-relative) | ❌ No guard |
| `preprocess_s2_meet.py` | Population Z-score: `(x - mean) / std` | Loaded from `teammate_inputs/s2_stats.json` | ✅ `std > 1e-7` guard |
| `preprocess_s2_jainam.py` | **Per-patch** Z-score: `(x - patch_mean) / patch_std` | Computed from each patch's own pixels | ✅ `np.maximum(std, 1e-6)` |
| `preprocess_s2_fateh.py` | **Per-patch** Z-score: `(x - patch_mean) / patch_std` | Computed from each patch's own pixels | ✅ `np.maximum(std, 1e-6)` |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Jainam and Fateh use per-patch normalization instead of population (dataset-wide) statistics.** This means each patch independently maps to roughly mean≈0, std≈1, losing inter-patch scale information. This is **incompatible with the training pipeline** that uses population statistics from `s2_stats.json`. Per-patch normalization is not wrong in isolation but cannot be mixed with scripts that use population normalization.

### A5. Missing/Invalid Value Handling

| Script | FileNotFoundError on missing band | NaN/Inf check | Shape validation |
|:-------|:---------------------------------|:-------------|:----------------|
| `preprocess_s2_adarsh.py` | `StopIteration` from `next(glob)` — unclear error | ❌ No | ❌ No |
| `preprocess_s2_meet.py` | ✅ `FileNotFoundError` with clear message | ✅ `np.isfinite` check | ✅ Shape assertion |
| `preprocess_s2_jainam.py` | ✅ `FileNotFoundError` | ❌ No explicit NaN check | ✅ Shape assertion |
| `preprocess_s2_fateh.py` | ✅ `FileNotFoundError` | ❌ No | ❌ No |

### A6. Output Type

| Script | Returns | Dtype |
|:-------|:--------|:------|
| `preprocess_s2_adarsh.py` | `np.ndarray` (3, 224, 224) | float32 |
| `preprocess_s2_meet.py` | `torch.Tensor` (3, 224, 224) | float32 |
| `preprocess_s2_jainam.py` | `torch.Tensor` (3, 224, 224) | float32 |
| `preprocess_s2_fateh.py` | `torch.Tensor` (3, 224, 224) | float32 |

> `preprocess_s2_adarsh.py` returns NumPy, while all others return Tensors. The Dataset class (`dataset_adarsh.py`) wraps the result with `torch.as_tensor(ms_tensor, dtype=torch.float32)` to handle this. ✅ Compatible.

### A7. S2 Canonical Candidate

**`preprocess_s2_meet.py`** ⭐  
Reasons: Correct channel order (B04→B03→B02), population statistics, division-by-zero guard, NaN/Inf validation, shape assertion, `FileNotFoundError` with clear messages, configurable `target_size`, hardcoded-path-free (uses `teammate_inputs/s2_stats.json` relative to script — still needs Phase 3 config centralization).

